+## Subtopics
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **SIMD Overhead:** Efficient SIMD implementation of range checking operations adds computational overhead, specifically requiring 16 SIMD operations to implement macrocell and particle range checking [S1].
-*   **Performance Degradation:** This overhead decreases performance by a factor of 1.38–2.28 compared to preprocessed versions of the data [S1].
-*   **Grid Bounds:** Some performance difference is attributed to grid bounds, where smaller bounds lead to fewer packet traversals [S1].
+### Frustum Culling
+Culls particles outside the view frustum. Sets it apart by using geometric bounds rather than simulation properties. Choose it for basic visibility optimization. [Frustum Culling](52-frustum-culling.md)
+
+### Ray/Box Culling
+Culls particles based on ray/axis-aligned bounding box intersections. Sets it apart by using ray-specific bounds. Choose it for ray-based acceleration. [Ray/Box Culling](52-ray-box-culling.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The macrocell check is $O(1)$ per macrocell and amortized over the contained particles [S1]. The particle check is $O(N)$ for $N$ particles in the macrocell, but reduces the constant factor by skipping intersection tests for out-of-range particles [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing $P_{min}$ and $P_{max}$ per macrocell [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the Thunder dataset, parameter range culling reduces total processing time by 43.00%, with a mean culling rate of 14.23% and a max culling rate of 43.06% [S1]. On the BulletTorso dataset, it reduces total processing time by 34.17%, with a mean culling rate of 3.17% and a max culling rate of 11.18% [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides flexibility for data analysis by allowing dynamic isolation of particle subsets without pre-processing [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The runtime overhead of SIMD range checks can degrade interactive performance compared to static preprocessed data [S1].
-*   **When to use:** It is used when interactive exploration of simulation data based on scalar parameters (e.g., temperature, velocity) is required [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The cost of range checking is amortized over the number of particles in a macrocell and is significantly less than the ray/sphere intersection tests [S1]. However, the 16 SIMD operations add computational overhead [S1, S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing min/max values for 8 parameters per macrocell [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   Thunder dataset: 43.06 fps (PR cull) vs 59.55 fps (Pre-crop) [S1, S2]. Total processing time reduced by 43.00%, mean culling rate 14.23%, max culling rate 43.06% [S2].
+    *   BulletTorso dataset: 11.18 fps (PR cull) vs 25.49 fps (Pre-crop) [S1, S2]. Total processing time reduced by 34.17%, mean culling rate 3.17%, max culling rate 11.18% [S2].
+    *   Performance degradation compared to preprocessed data: Factor of 1.38–2.28 [S1, S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides interactive flexibility for data analysis by allowing runtime range changes [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Adds computational overhead due to SIMD operations, resulting in lower frame rates compared to pre-cropped data [S1, S2].
+*   **When to use:** When interactive exploration of specific simulation properties is required.
+*   **When not to use:** When maximum performance is needed and the particle subset is static (use pre-cropping instead) [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in "A Coherent Grid Traversal Approach to Visualizing Particle Data" by Thiago Ize et al. [S1].
-*   **History:** It was developed as part of the Particle Coherent Grid Traversal (PCGT) framework to support interactive visualization of large-scale particle simulations [S1].
-*   **Usage:** It is used in scientific visualization for analyzing particle-based simulations, such as fluid dynamics or astrophysical data, where researchers need to filter particles based on physical properties [S1].
+Parameter Range Culling was introduced in the context of coherent grid traversal for visualizing particle-based simulation data [S1, S2]. It was developed to allow investigators to isolate particles with specific parameter values (e.g., density, velocity) to gain insight into simulation behavior [S1, S2]. The method is part of a broader suite of culling techniques including frustum culling and ray/box culling [S1].
 
